WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
The first film gave every bird the same intake.

2
00:00:07.480 --> 00:00:12.200
If breeding can cost a bird its future, why do birds that lay more eggs often

3
00:00:12.200 --> 00:00:16.720
survive better? At first glance, breeding looks good for them.

4
00:00:17.683 --> 00:00:19.523
Draw the line for a poor bird.

5
00:00:20.823 --> 00:00:24.103
Its intake is small, so its line sits close to the corner.

6
00:00:25.423 --> 00:00:26.803
A rich bird takes in more.

7
00:00:28.123 --> 00:00:31.163
Its line sits further out, with the same downward slope.

8
00:00:32.543 --> 00:00:33.763
Same split, more of both.

9
00:00:34.833 --> 00:00:36.773
Now fill in a whole population.

10
00:00:38.153 --> 00:00:41.773
Each bird is a dot on its own line, and every line slopes down.

11
00:00:43.093 --> 00:00:46.033
Yet birds that take in more sit further out on both axes.

12
00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:48.953
So the cloud of dots slopes up.

13
00:00:49.867 --> 00:00:53.587
Van Noordwijk and de Jong made this point in 1986.

14
00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:59.187
People with large incomes tend to have bigger houses and newer cars.

15
00:01:00.547 --> 00:01:04.367
Yet for any one income, money spent on the house is not there for the car.

16
00:01:05.450 --> 00:01:09.590
So a positive natural correlation does not refute a trade-off.

17
00:01:10.850 --> 00:01:12.810
It may simply reflect who took in more.

18
00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.870
To test the trade-off, change breeding effort on purpose.

19
00:01:18.283 --> 00:01:21.663
Here is how. Take a set of nests.

20
00:01:22.883 --> 00:01:26.983
At random, add two chicks to half of them, and leave the other half alone.

21
00:01:28.383 --> 00:01:31.383
Parents of enlarged broods now have more mouths to feed.

22
00:01:32.467 --> 00:01:37.387
Because a coin chose the nests, rich and poor parents are balanced between the

23
00:01:37.387 --> 00:01:38.387
groups on average.

24
00:01:39.647 --> 00:01:44.507
A later difference in parent survival can then be laid on the extra chicks, allowing

25
00:01:44.507 --> 00:01:45.507
for chance.

26
00:01:46.267 --> 00:01:47.987
Now a question for you.

27
00:01:49.247 --> 00:01:51.587
Suppose extra chicks cost parents some survival.

28
00:01:52.947 --> 00:01:56.267
What should happen to the parents of enlarged broods by the next spring?

29
00:01:57.587 --> 00:01:59.827
Will more of them come back, fewer, or the same?

30
00:02:06.300 --> 00:02:10.400
A meta-analysis gathered such studies across 30 bird species.

31
00:02:11.740 --> 00:02:15.740
On average, parents of enlarged broods survived a little less well.

32
00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:19.040
The cost per extra chick was uncertain.

33
00:02:20.220 --> 00:02:23.680
And a cost could also fall on later breeding, not survival.

34
00:02:24.980 --> 00:02:29.320
In the same data, birds with naturally larger clutches survived better.

35
00:02:30.540 --> 00:02:34.300
Two opposite signs, which fits the cloud, though it does not prove it.

36
00:02:35.283 --> 00:02:39.823
So a natural correlation alone cannot show a trade-off, or rule one out.

37
00:02:41.263 --> 00:02:43.783
Birds that take in more can do better at everything.

38
00:02:45.243 --> 00:02:48.423
Adding chicks at random tests what extra breeding costs.

39
00:02:49.663 --> 00:02:52.363
And each bird may still face its own downward line.
